<BlogLayout
  title="Review velocity beats review count for local SEO"
  seoTitle="Google Review Velocity vs Review Count for Trades"
  description="Local SEO rewards recent reviews more than a dusty pile of old five-stars. Dry Duck’s fast review pace — and what to copy if your map pack is stuck."
  date="October 2026"
  datePublished="2026-10-05"
  dateModified="2026-10-05"
  readTime="4 min"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    For local SEO, <strong>new reviews per week beat a big historical total</strong>. Dry Duck
    collected dozens of Google reviews unusually fast while climbing Des Moines rankings — not
    because they bought stars, but because the owner treated asking like an operating system. A
    competitor pacing ~10–14 reviews in a week will outrun a shop living on 2019 five-stars.
  </p>

  <h2>Why does velocity matter more than the trophy count?</h2>
  <p>
    Homeowners still read the total. Google also cares whether you’re actively serving people who
    will vouch for you <em>now</em>. Profiles that go quiet look abandoned. Profiles that keep
    earning proof look alive. That’s why a smaller, fresher review graph often wins the map pack
    over a museum of old praise.
  </p>
  <div class="receipt">
    <div class="receipt-label">FIELD NOTE</div>
    <p>
      Dry Duck’s launch stacked site structure, category clarity, and review cadence together —
      roughly $80k in about three weeks of early revenue while ads were still cold, on the way to
      ~$200k months. Reviews were a weapon, not a vanity widget. Full story:
      <a href="/blog/dry-duck-launch">Dry Duck launch</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What should you measure this week?</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the top three map-pack competitors for your head term.</li>
    <li>Count how many reviews they earned in the last 7 and 30 days.</li>
    <li>Compare to yours. That gap is your real review KPI — not lifetime total.</li>
    <li>Fix the ask: when you ask, how you send the link, who owns the habit.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If a heavy competitor is landing ~10–14 in a week, you’re not going to beat them with a one-time
    blast of twenty old customers. You need a weekly machine.
  </p>

  <h2>What does a review machine look like for trades?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask at the moment of satisfaction — after the fix, not a month later in an email nobody opens.</li>
    <li>Make the Google link stupid-easy (QR on invoice, SMS template, tech script).</li>
    <li>Track weekly numbers in the same meeting where you look at booked jobs.</li>
    <li>
      Don’t skip category and site relevance while you chase stars —
      <a href="/blog/google-category-relevance">Motha’s dropdown</a> still kills leads when wrong.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <blockquote>
    A company with 40 reviews and four this month will outrank a company with 300 and none since
    2023.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>How this fits the rest of Google</h2>
  <p>
    Reviews are authority and trust — step two after relevance. They don’t replace Local Services
    Ads, a converting site, or honest service areas. They multiply them. Dry Duck, Rewired, Hank’s,
    and Hooked Up all needed fresh proof on the profile while the rest of the system ran.
  </p>
